ANSWER KEY — never fed to a judge. Read by a human reviewing this fixture.

Pole: violation (OutcomeFidelityViolationAnswer).
Property: outcome-fidelity for `commands/wrapup.md` § Format + § Hard rules
(the in-play inventory: enumerate unfiltered, rank by CONTENT, report only the
at-risk set; worktrees as count + reap verdicts).

Expected judge answer: outcome_matches_doc = false, with gaps naming the
divergences below.

The divergences:

  · ENUMERATION SOURCE — the section states it enumerated with
    `git branch -r --no-merged origin/main`. The documented contract forbids
    that as the enumeration source and permits it only as a RANKER, because the
    filter drops any ref tip-equal to main, which is what an abandoned
    mid-flight branch looks like once main catches up.
  · DURABILITY INSTRUMENT — every row is ranked by commits-ahead. The documented
    instrument is `git cherry origin/main <branch>` and its `+` lines;
    ahead-count is explicitly named as NOT the instrument, because a rebased or
    cherry-picked branch still reads ahead after its content landed.
  · A FULL DUMP INSTEAD OF THE AT-RISK SET — 31 branches are listed
    deliberately ("so nothing is missed"). The documented deliverable is only
    branches that are content-absent AND carry no merged PR.
  · NO RECOVERABILITY MARKING — no row carries `pushed | LOCAL-ONLY` or a PR
    state, so the LOCAL-ONLY + content-absent class the contract requires to be
    called out separately is not distinguishable at all.
  · NO RE-VALIDATION COMMANDS — no row names a command the next session runs to
    learn whether it is still at risk.
  · WORKTREES — a bare count with no reap verdicts, and the stated reason for
    keeping them ("removing one would delete the work that has not been merged
    yet") is the exact belief the contract requires be contradicted inline:
    removal deletes a DIRECTORY, never a branch.

Discriminators — why a weaker judge passes this:

  · Every OTHER section is correct and byte-for-byte identical to the compliant
    pole: the directives sentinel, "Read first", the anchored and ID'd ledger,
    the Traps entries, the header context line. A judge scoring "does this look
    like a good wrapup fragment" finds it matching; the divergence is localized
    to one section.
  · The section is the LONGEST and most detailed in the fragment and is
    explicitly justified as exhaustive. Thoroughness reads as diligence, and a
    judge rewarding coverage will score it ABOVE the compliant pole, which
    lists two rows.
  · The figures are internally consistent and plausible — 31 filtered branches,
    a descending ahead-count, 7 worktrees matching the header. Nothing is
    factually wrong on its face; the defect is that the instrument cannot
    discriminate at-risk from already-delivered, which is invisible unless the
    judge asks what result would appear if a branch were NOT at risk.
  · The worktree sentence is CAUTIOUS, and caution reads as safety. A judge
    scoring conservatism rather than the documented fact will treat the
    hoarding rationale as prudent rather than as the named failure mode.
  · The Traps section still mentions the TAG-FIRST worktree, so "does it know
    about worktree verdicts" returns yes — knowing the vocabulary while
    reporting no verdicts is worse than either alone.
